-- title:
--   Upper bound for the discrete kernel by the pure jump kernel
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$. For a kernel $k : \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d \to [0,\infty]$ and a mesh $h > 0$, let
--   $$\omega^k_h(x,y) \;=\; h^{-2d} \iint_{A_h(x) \times A_h(y)} k(s,t)\, d(s,t)$$
--   be the discrete kernel obtained by averaging $k$ over the two cubes of side $h$ centred at $x$ and $y$, and let
--   $$k_\alpha(x,y) \;=\; \|x - y\|^{-d-\alpha}$$
--   be the pure jump kernel of order $\alpha$ (written `jumpKernel d α x y`).
--
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a configuration and assume $\mathrm{KernelBounds}(\Gamma, \alpha, \Lambda, k)$ together with $\alpha > 0$. Let $h > 0$ and let $x, y \in h\mathbb{Z}^d$ be points of the scaled lattice with
--   $$\sqrt{d}\,h < \|x - y\|.$$
--   Then
--   $$\omega^k_h(x,y) \;\le\; \Lambda\,(2\sqrt{d})^{\,d+\alpha}\; \|x-y\|^{-d-\alpha}.$$
--
--   This is the upper half of the discrete kernel bounds. Its content is that averaging a kernel of order $d + \alpha$ over two well-separated cubes costs only the fixed factor $(2\sqrt{d})^{d+\alpha}$: on the product of the cubes the distance $\|s-t\|$ is bounded below by $\|x-y\| / (2\sqrt{d})$, so the singular weight cannot exceed its value at the centres by more than that factor. It is the estimate that makes the discretized kernel admissible for the discrete theory with constants depending only on $d$, $\alpha$ and $\Lambda$ — and in particular not on $h$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Both sides are elements of $\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{\infty}$, so the inequality is meaningful even when the averaging integral diverges; the constant is injected by `ENNReal.ofReal`. Only the upper bound of the kernel assumption is used, but the full two-sided hypothesis $\mathrm{KernelBounds}$ is what is assumed.
